## 1. Scope of the corpus

**S1: 10-K filings only** · Status: By design
The corpus contains annual reports (Form 10-K) only: no 10-Q, 8-K or proxy statements. Part III items (executive compensation, director details) are usually "incorporated by reference" to the proxy statement, so those details are not available. Real-time data (for example today's stock price) is never in a 10-K; the answer stage must reply that it is not available.

**S2: Fiscal-year labels follow each company's own fiscal-year name** · Status: Resolved (relabeled); the limitation remains
21 files were relabeled with `fix_years.py` after `check_data.py` compared each file's label with the years inside its data: Home Depot names its fiscal year by the calendar year in which it starts (2017–2026 → 2016–2025), Workday by the year in which it ends (2016–2025 → 2017–2026), and one Southwest file (2026 → 2025). Originals are in `STAGE_1/chunks_backup_yearfix/`; the change log is `STAGE_1/eval/year_fixes.json`.
Ranges: most companies FY2016–2025; Dell, Donaldson, Intuit, Kennametal, Microsoft, Nike, Nvidia, Parker Hannifin, Salesforce, Walmart and Workday FY2017–2026. The same label is not the same calendar period across companies (for example, Walmart FY2026 ends on 31 Jan 2026). "Latest" means the newest fiscal year available for that company. The year-detection heuristic (column years in the tables plus "fiscal YYYY" mentions, with exceptions for start-year naming and programme names such as "Fiscal 2020 Restructuring") should be re-checked when new companies are added.

**S3: Company names are stored as they appear in the source files** · Status: Mitigated
Stored names include typos and mixed styles ("Rockwell Auomation", "KImberly_Clark", "WATTS WATER TECHNOLOGIES", "Google" for Alphabet, "META", "SalesForce"). The retriever maps tickers and alternate names (`EXTRA_ALIASES`) and shows clean names in results (`DISPLAY_NAMES`). A future rebuild should normalise the stored names.

